  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given two arrays AAA and BBB, each of size NNN.\n\nLet PPP denote a permutation length NNN. The permutation PPP is said to be good, if you can make an array CCC where:\n\n\nCi=Ai+BPiC_i = A_i + B_{P_i}Ci​=Ai​+BPi​​ **or** Ci=Ai−BPiC_i = A_i - B_{P_i}Ci​=Ai​−BPi​​, for all (1≤i≤N)(1\\le i \\le N)(1≤i≤N);\nAll elements of CCC are **equal**.\nOut of the N!N!N! possible permutations, find the count of good permutations.\n\nSince the count can be huge, output it modulo 109+710^9+7109+7.\n\nNote that a permutation of length NNN consists of all integers from 111 to NNN exactly once.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case consists of a single integer NNN - the size of arrays.\nThe second line of each test case consists of NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2,\\ldots , A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, the elements of array A.\nThe third line of each test case consists of N space-separated integers B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2,\\ldots , B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​, the elements of array B.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the count of good permutations, modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\leq N \\leq 10^51≤N≤105",
      "−109≤Ai≤109-10^9 \\le A_i \\leq 10^9−109≤Ai​≤109",
      "−109≤Bi≤109-10^9 \\le B_i \\leq 10^9−109≤Bi​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3\n10 5 2\n-7 -4 -1\n3\n6 7 8\n-6 5 7\n6\n2 6 5 5 5 4\n7 -5 4 -4 -4 -3\n4\n2 2 2 2\n3 3 -3 -3",
        "output": "1\n2\n6\n24",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The only good permutation is [3,2,1][3,2,1][3,2,1]. We construct CCC as following:\n\nC1=A1+BP1=A1+B3=10−1=9C_1 = A_1 + B_{P_1} = A_1 + B_3 = 10 - 1 = 9C1​=A1​+BP1​​=A1​+B3​=10−1=9;\nC2=A2−BP2=A2−B2=5−(−4)=9C_2 = A_2 - B_{P_2} = A_2 - B_2 = 5 - (-4) = 9C2​=A2​−BP2​​=A2​−B2​=5−(−4)=9;\nC3=A3−BP3=A3−B1=2−(−7)=9C_3 = A_3 - B_{P_3} = A_3 - B_1 = 2 - (-7) = 9C3​=A3​−BP3​​=A3​−B1​=2−(−7)=9.\n\nHere all elements of array CCC are equal. It can be shown that for all other permutations, it is impossible to create such an array CCC. Thus, the count of good permutations is 111.\nTest case 222: The good permutations are [2,1,3],[3,1,2][2,1,3], [3, 1, 2][2,1,3],[3,1,2]. We construct CCC as following using [2,1,3][2, 1, 3][2,1,3]:\n\nC1=A1−BP1=A1−B2=6−5=1C_1 = A_1 - B_{P_1} = A_1 - B_2 = 6 - 5 = 1C1​=A1​−BP1​​=A1​−B2​=6−5=1;\nC2=A2+BP2=A2+B1=7+(−6)=1C_2 = A_2 + B_{P_2} = A_2 + B_1 = 7 + (-6) = 1C2​=A2​+BP2​​=A2​+B1​=7+(−6)=1;\nC3=A3−BP3=A3−B3=8−7=1C_3 = A_3 - B_{P_3} = A_3 - B_3 = 8 - 7 = 1C3​=A3​−BP3​​=A3​−B3​=8−7=1.\n\nHere all elements of array CCC are equal. It can be shown that for [3,1,2][3, 1, 2][3,1,2], it is possible to create such an array CCC. Thus, the count of good permutations is 222."
      }
    ]
  },
